maria: Heads-up, I'm opening a tiny docs PR that adds a compact repro + cheat-sheet for handling streaming -> non-stream fallback when clients see repeated 429s during long responses. PR draft #5012.

liam: Sounds useful. Can you keep examples concise for Node + Python? Also include the Retry-After handling and a one-liner remediation map.

maria: Yes. Node + Python included. Here's the Node idea (very compact):

```js
// try stream, on repeated 429 -> backoff & batch
let attempts = 0
try {
  const stream = await client.stream(payload)
  await stream.pipeTo(ui)
} catch (err) {
  if (err.status === 429) {
    attempts++
    const wait = backoffMs(attempts) + jitterMs()
    await sleep(wait)
    if (attempts >= 2) {
      // fallback to single request (no stream)
      const res = await client.create(payloadNoStream)
      render(res)
    }
  } else { throw err }
}
```

sam: Add guidance to prefer reading Retry-After if present and cap retries. Recommend full jitter plus exponential base and max cap. Also note trimming context if you hit 409/413.

maria: added a short paragraph on Retry-After priority, then jitter recommendation and caps. Also included a tiny Python example showing error.code handling from our SDK: 

```py
# SDK raises typed errors
try:
  r = sdk.stream_create(payload)
except SDKError as e:
  if e.code == 'RateLimited':
    sleep(backoff(attempts) + jitter())
    if attempts >= 2:
      r = sdk.create(payload_no_stream)
```

jen: QA volunteer here. I can run a dev-sim that emits configurable 429 bursts and paste the captured logs. Please add the exact curl command for the sim.

maria: done. I added the sim curl and a tiny node harness that logs the sequence (status / header values / backoff). Curl example: 

```bash
# dev-sim: burst 429 on /v1/chat
curl -X POST "https://dev-sim.redwood.internal/simulate" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -d '{"scenario":"burst-429","target":"/v1/chat/completions","burst_rate":4}'
```

liam: Please include an "expected signals" checklist so folks know when to apply this pattern vs escalate: e.g., sporadic 429s during peak tokens -> client backoff; sustained 429s across routes -> infra/engineer triage.

maria: added checklist and a 1-line remediation matrix: 429 -> backoff + batch/fallback; 408 -> increase client socket/stream timeout; 413/409 -> trim prompt or use longer-window model; 500/503 -> retry with capped attempts.

sam: small add: call out Dedicated vs Private nuance. Dedicated often shows pool-saturation 429s and benefits from route-level throttles/burst settings; Private may have local proxies that need operator rule changes. Keep it compact.

maria: included a short paragraph about Dedicated/Private differences and a pointer: see err-ref/429 and err-ref/context-limit links.

jen: QA plan: run sim + harness, validate that after two 429s the client falls back and that Retry-After headers shorten the backoff loops. I'll post logs in this thread when done.

maria: thanks! Also added an "escalate to eng-runtime" checklist: consistent >1% 429s across traffic, per-route 50th->95th latency divergence after rollouts, or observed loss of streaming resume tokens.

liam: tiny nit: include reference to our SDK backoff util and suggest full jitter implementation. Also add one-liner about partial-stream resume being best-effort and may not be supported on older model variants.

maria: updated. PR ready for review, requesting @liam @sam.

